    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The originalist argument for an individual right to bear arms, as seen in District of Columbia v. Heller (2008), and other cases, relies on a method of interpretation known as textualism or strict originalism. This approach involves analyzing the words and phrases used in the Constitution's text to determine their original meaning and intent at the time of ratification. \n\nFor example, the phrase \"the right of the people\" is interpreted to mean an individual right, rather than a collective one, and the use of the word \"arms\" is seen as referring to personal firearms, rather than supplies or equipment. In contrast, living constitutionalists argue that the individual tobbe reads the words and phrases in context, taking into account the intended purpose and circumstances, and that the amendment's text and history suggest a collective right.",
